connects[1] id=cancelled-and-shipped-the-same-day
TITLE: One company cancelled a frontier model and shipped another on the same day
ITEMS: ['openai-pacing-and-departures', 'frontier-releases-week']
TOPICS: ['openai', 'anthropic', 'google-deepmind', 'evals', 'alignment', 'agents']
STORYLINES: ['pacing-frontier-ai']
 para1 :: Both developments are dated 29 September. OpenAI cancelled the October release of GPT-6.1 Astra, with its head of safety systems saying the model "didn't quite meet the bar in terms of staying within scope and authorisation", and shipped GPT-6.1 Sol and launched "dots" at DevDay. The ABC reports that the cancellation "comes ahead of OpenAI's developer conference in San Francisco". Anthropic had shipped Claude Sonnet 5.5 the day before, and Google shipped Gemini 4 Argon the day after.
 para2 :: The two sit either side of the same stated standard. OpenAI published its safety-case proposals on 29 September, covering alignment training, containment and monitoring before frontier reinforcement-learning training continues, and the published account says the recommendations are already being rolled out internally. No safety case was published alongside any of the three releases that week, and no lab other than Anthropic has named an external evaluator with standing access.
 para3 :: The one release that moved in the opposite direction on guardrails was Google's. SecurityWeek reports Gemini 4 Argon went to vetted cyber defenders first without the cyber guardrails applied to general releases.
